<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#These%20formulas%20describe%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20for%20constructing%20an%20orthogonal%20set%20of%20nonzero%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20which%20spans%20the%20same%20subspace%20as%20a%20given%20independent%20set> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#These%20formulas%20describe%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20for%20constructing%20an%20orthogonal%20set%20of%20nonzero%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20which%20spans%20the%20same%20subspace%20as%20a%20given%20independent%20set> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "These formulas describe the Gram-Schmidt process for constructing an orthogonal set of nonzero elements $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ which spans the same subspace as a given independent set" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "We can also convert this to an orthonormal basis by normalizing each element $y_{i}$ , that is, by dividing it by its norm." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "When the orthogonalization theorem is applied to this sequence it yields another sequence of polynomials $y_0, y_1, y_2, \\ldots$" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "given by $\\varphi_{n} = y_{n}/\\|y_{n}\\|$ are called the normalized Legendre polynomials." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20x_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20a%20basis%20for%20a%20finite-dimensional%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20then%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20an%20orthogonal%20basis%20for%20the%20same%20space.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20x_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20a%20basis%20for%20a%20finite-dimensional%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20then%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20an%20orthogonal%20basis%20for%20the%20same%20space.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "if $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{k}$ is a basis for a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then $y_{1}, \\ldots, y_{k}$ is an orthogonal basis for the same space." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20%24y_1%27%2C%20y_2%27%2C%20%5Cldots%24%20%2C%20is%20another%20sequence%20of%20elements%20in%20%24V%24%20satisfying%20properties%20%28a%29%20and%20%28b%29%2C%20then%20for%20each%20%24k%24%20there%20is%20a%20scalar%20%24c_k%24%20such%20that%20%24y_k%27%20%3D%20c_k%20y_k%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20%24y_1%27%2C%20y_2%27%2C%20%5Cldots%24%20%2C%20is%20another%20sequence%20of%20elements%20in%20%24V%24%20satisfying%20properties%20%28a%29%20and%20%28b%29%2C%20then%20for%20each%20%24k%24%20there%20is%20a%20scalar%20%24c_k%24%20such%20that%20%24y_k%27%20%3D%20c_k%20y_k%24%20.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "if $y_1', y_2', \\ldots$ , is another sequence of elements in $V$ satisfying properties (a) and (b), then for each $k$ there is a scalar $c_k$ such that $y_k' = c_k y_k$ ." .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "if the first k elements $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}$ are independent, then the corresponding elements $y_{1},\\ldots,y_{k}$ are nonzero" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> <http://example.org/ch15/file> "15.13.md" .
<http://example.org/ch15/anchor/15.13.md#is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> <http://example.org/ch15/quote> "is called the projection of x along y." .
